          "field": "description",
          "page_id": "page-4e0a2e9d4d7ecd74cb1c518e",
          "qa_findings": [],
          "review_status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "segment_id": "seg-2eb6c5f4d25b86b962c96f9a",
          "segment_index": 12,
          "source_segment_id": "seg-2eb6c5f4d25b86b962c96f9a",
          "source_text_sha256": "5d6d8f9b7d55c269239bb262a5414dedcb114e2aa0987cb776327d86951e7938",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: A witness is intrigued by an observation made on August 22, 2012 at 10:19 PM. He observed the passage of a bright point that, after an initial straight trajectory, makes jerky movements from right to left before disappearing. The witness's description (appearance and direction of movement) corresponds, within a minute, to the observation of the passage of the International Space Station (ISS). The temporary extinction corresponds to variations in the sun's reflections on the station during its rotation. The jerky right-to-left movements are a perceptual illusion known as \"autokinetic illusion\" (see pages 26 and 30 in http://www.cnes-geipan.fr/typo3conf/ext/dam_frontend/pushfile.php?docID=9932; this illusion occurs when the bright point being followed is no longer surrounded by fixed references, for example, when it crosses a starless area of the sky. This case is classified as \"A\": observation of the International Space Station (ISS) with autokinetic illusion.",
          "translation_text_sha256": "0d5f1b32ecd56c090b967448cb7336491a7b6c10de787f4f322721ba90dad6a3"
        }
      ],
